Roast.pet Weird Builder Projects =E2=80=94 5 useful projects

This run reviewed 20+ plausible candidates and deduplicated against earlier
Roast.pet scans. The strongest new find is
LostPetInitiative/study_winter_22_23: a student/research repo with a
pretrained YOLOv7 pet-face detector that returns both a pet face box and 3
landmarks (two eyes + nose), plus an alignment script. This is almost
exactly the missing deterministic preprocessing primitive before generating
Roast.pet mouth/eye variants.
1) LostPetInitiative/study_winter_22_23

URL: https://github.com/LostPetInitiative/study_winter_22_23
Creator: LostPetInitiative / MDS student project; model archive credits Chi
Chon Ng and Hon Sang Leong.
Activity: 149 commits; project/model archive published 2023.
License: no clear repo license surfaced in this run =E2=80=94 treat reuse r=
ights as
UNKNOWN until checked; do not ship copied code/weights commercially yet.
Solves: dog/cat face detection, canonical crop and alignment before sprite
generation.
Route: Python 3.8 + PyTorch/YOLOv7. Pretrained yolov7-pet-face.pt is
archived on Zenodo (~73.5 MB). Inference outputs bbox + confidence + 3
landmarks; face_alignment.py consumes detector outputs.
Hardware: CPU possible; GPU optional/preferred for throughput.
Evidence: ~15k-image pet-face dataset assembled from AnimalWeb, cat
datasets, TsingHua Dog and Dogs vs Cats, with bbox + two-eye/nose
landmarks; pretrained weights publicly archived on Zenodo DOI
10.5281/zenodo.7607110.
Work replaced: pet-specific face detector, eye/nose locator, canonical
crop/alignment glue =E2=80=94 potentially several days to weeks and avoids =
trying
human MediaPipe on pets.
Difficulty: LOW-MEDIUM.
Maintenance risk: MEDIUM; older YOLOv7 stack and uncertain license.
Experiment: run 100 ugly customer-like dog/cat photos; record
face-detection rate, landmark plausibility and whether aligned output makes
generated 9-mouth packs more identity-consistent. Compare directly with
simple object crop.
Decision: COPY MECHANISM / BENCHMARK NOW; verify licensing before
code/weight reuse.
2) kairess/dog_face_detector

URL: https://github.com/kairess/dog_face_detector
Creator: kairess; model credited upstream to Doggie-smile.
Activity: tiny 5-commit hobby repo; 68 stars / 24 forks.
License: no license confirmed in this run =E2=80=94 treat as UNKNOWN.
Solves: dog face rectangle + 6 facial landmarks with dlib.
Route: Python + dlib + OpenCV + imutils + NumPy; pretrained detector files
are included.
Hardware: CPU-friendly old-school CV stack.
Evidence: runnable image/video demo; an adjacent personal blog reports the
detector was trained on >9,000 dog heads and demonstrates repurposing its
eye/nose landmarks for alignment.
Work replaced: dog-specific landmark bootstrap and a cheap fallback
detector.
Difficulty: LOW.
Maintenance risk: HIGH due age/unknown model provenance; use as
benchmark/fallback, not core.
Experiment: same 100-photo set as LostPet detector; compare failure modes
and CPU latency.
Decision: WATCH / COPY MECHANISM.
3) bunn-io/rembg-web

URL: https://github.com/bunn-io/rembg-web
Creator: bunn-io.
Activity/release: npm/API docs v1.0.2; current GitHub crawl.
License: MIT code; verify each selected model=E2=80=99s own license before
commercial deployment.
Solves: browser-native cutout without adopting a whole background-removal
application.
Route: npm install @bunnio/rembg-web onnxruntime-web. About 8 KB gzipped
library excluding runtime/model. Supports U2Net/U2NetP/ISNet/Silueta,
custom model paths, caching and mask-only output.
Hardware: WASM baseline; WebNN/WebGPU where usable.
Evidence: package docs, examples, model/session caching and published
npm/CDN package.
Work replaced: custom ONNX browser loader, model registry, session/cache
management and cutout API.
Difficulty: LOW.
Maintenance risk: LOW-MEDIUM; browser acceleration varies and model
licensing differs.
Experiment: benchmark u2netp vs existing Alphaveil/Cutout Studio on 30
fur-heavy photos, including first-load size, repeat latency and edge score.
Decision: REUSE NOW if the chosen model license is commercial-safe.
4) thangtn-foobla/background-removal

URL: https://github.com/thangtn-foobla/background-removal
Creator: thangtn-foobla.
Activity: one-commit microrepo, crawled last month.
License: not established in surfaced evidence =E2=80=94 treat as UNKNOWN.
Solves: production details around browser ONNX cutout: iOS fallback, worker
execution, cancellation, warmup, model hot-swap/rollback and IndexedDB
cache.
Route: Vite/JS + ONNX Runtime Web; U2Net 176 MB, MODNet 25.9 MB or
quantized MODNet 6.6 MB.
Hardware: WebGPU preferred; deliberately forces WASM on iOS/iPadOS where
ONNX WebGPU is unreliable.
Evidence: concrete service API and documented implementation, including
versioned model URLs and rollback.
Work replaced: boring but important browser inference lifecycle code.
Difficulty: LOW.
Maintenance risk: MEDIUM because it is tiny/unmaintained-looking.
Experiment: copy only its device-routing and model-lifecycle ideas into a
disposable page; test iPhone Safari plus desktop Chrome with cancellation
and repeat runs.
Decision: COPY MECHANISM.
5) crdrury/Unity-Rhubarb-Lip-Syncer

URL: https://github.com/crdrury/Unity-Rhubarb-Lip-Syncer
Creator: crdrury.
Activity: 79 stars; recently crawled.
License: verify before reuse; not confirmed in surfaced evidence this run.
Solves: automatic conversion of Rhubarb output into editable 2D sprite
animation.
Route: Unity + Rhubarb; mouth sprites named by Rhubarb shape and generated
into an animation track. Transcript can be supplied to Rhubarb for better
timing.
Hardware: CPU; no inference GPU required.
Evidence: documented 2D workflow and walkthrough/demo path.
Work replaced: not our renderer, but validates the exact mouth-sprite state
abstraction and provides a reference for editable animation generation.
Difficulty: LOW to study, HIGH if we foolishly adopt Unity.
Maintenance risk: LOW as a mechanism, HIGH as a direct dependency.
Experiment: do not integrate Unity. Compare its generated mouth-state
timing/hold behavior with our Rhubarb-crumble/LipSyncify implementation and
copy any useful timing semantics.
Decision: COPY MECHANISM, NOT STACK.
New architectural consequence

The LostPet detector is the material find. Our cheap deterministic V1 can
now have a pet-native alignment stage:

customer photo -> pet face bbox + eye/nose landmarks -> canonical aligned
pet crop -> cutout/upscale -> generate 9 mouth + 3 eye states -> Rhubarb ->
transparent pet animation -> captions/template -> Etsy/MindAR.

This is better than using human MediaPipe as the locator and hoping it
generalizes. It also gives a measurable quality gate: if face
confidence/landmark geometry is poor, request another customer image before
spending generation/render compute.
TOP 5 THINGS WE SHOULD NOT BUILD OURSELVES

   1. Pet face detection/alignment: benchmark LostPetInitiative=E2=80=99s
   pretrained detector first.
   2. Generic dog landmark fallback: existing dlib dog-face hobby models
   already exist.
   3. Browser ONNX model/session/cache plumbing: rembg-web already packages
   it.
   4. iOS inference fallback/cancellation/model lifecycle: copy the thangtn
   microrepo=E2=80=99s mechanism.
   5. Rhubarb-to-sprite animation semantics: multiple independent wrappers
   already solve it.

Most surprising hobby project: LostPetInitiative/study_winter_22_23. It has
zero stars in the surfaced result but 149 commits, a 15k-image pet-face
dataset and archived pretrained dog/cat face+landmark weights =E2=80=94 far=
 more
useful to Roast.pet than another generic avatar framework.

Fastest integration win: download the LostPet pretrained detector into a
disposable benchmark and test whether canonical eye/nose alignment improves
mouth-pack identity consistency. If yes, this becomes the quality-gate/crop
primitive before any image generation.

Repo/author worth following: LostPetInitiative and the Chi Chon Ng / Hon
Sang Leong model archive. Their work is unusually aligned with the exact
pet-specific preprocessing gap.

